 Hello! I'd be happy to help you create a PHP custom function named `smart_sort_array`. Here's how you can define this function based on your requirements:

```php
function smart_sort_array($set, $order = 'ASC') {
    // Check if set and order are valid
    if (!is_array($set) || count($set) <= 1) {
        return $set;
    }

    $size = count($set);

    for ($i = 0; $i < $size - 1; $i++) {
        for ($j = $i + 1; $j < $size; $j++) {
            $shouldSwap = false;

            if ($order == 'ASC') {
                if ($set[$i]['age'] > $set[$j]['age']) {
                    $shouldSwap = true;
                }
            } else if ($order == 'DESC') {
                if ($set[$i]['age'] < $set[$j]['age']) {
                    $shouldSwap = true;
                }
            }

            if ($shouldSwap) {
                $temp = $set[$i];
                $set[$i] = $set[$j];
                $set[$j] = $temp;
            }
        }
    }

    return $set;
}
```

This function accepts an associative array named `$set` as its first argument and an optional string argument `$order` with two possible values ('ASC' for ascending or 'DESC' for descending order). It sorts the elements in the array `$set` based on their 'age' value using the simple switching method (bubble sort). If the `$order` argument is not provided, the default value will be 'ASC'.

 In JavaScript, a Promise is an object that represents the eventual completion or failure of an asynchronous task. It's like a container for a value that may not be available right away but will be resolved at some point in the future.

Imagine you order a pizza from your favorite pizzeria. When you place an order, you don't get the pizza immediately; it takes some time to prepare and cook it. Instead, you receive a promise that your pizza will be ready soon. This promise holds the value of your pizza once it's prepared.

Similarly, when writing JavaScript code, you can use Promises to handle tasks that take some time to complete, such as fetching data from a server or reading and writing files. Instead of blocking the execution flow by using callbacks or synchronous methods, you can return a Promise and let the JavaScript engine manage the execution.

A Promise has three states:

1. Pending: The initial state before the Promise is either resolved or rejected.
2. Resolved: The Promise has a value associated with it.
3. Rejected: The Promise encountered an error or failure.

When creating a new Promise, you can specify a function called the executor, which contains two functions: resolve() and reject(). These functions are used to change the Promise's state to either resolved or rejected. Once a Promise is resolved or rejected, its state cannot be changed.

Here's a simple example of creating and handling a Promise:

```javascript
let myPromise = new Promise(function(resolve, reject) {
  // Some asynchronous task goes here
  setTimeout(() => {
    if (someCondition) {
      resolve("The condition is true!");
    } else {
      reject("The condition is false.");
    }
  }, 1000);
});

myPromise.then(value => {
  console.log("Success:", value);
}).catch(error => {
  console.log("Error:", error);
});
```

In this example, the Promise represents the result of an asynchronous task with a delay of one second. Depending on the condition, it will either be resolved with a success message or rejected with an error message. The then() method is used to handle the resolved Promise, while catch() handles any errors.

