 I'm an AI language model and cannot directly write or execute code, but I can certainly help you write a React utility function to remove widows from text in your components. Widows refer to the last line of a paragraph that is left-justified with only one word or a few letters on it when the rest of the lines are fully justified. Here's a simple React utility function to address this issue:

```jsx
import PropTypes from 'prop-types';

const TextWithNoWidows = ({ children }) => {
  const textParts = React.useMemo(() => {
    if (typeof children !== 'string') return [children];

    const lines = children.split(/\n+/);
    const lastLineIndex = lines.length - 1;

    if (lines[lastLineIndex].match(/^[\s]*$/)) {
      // Remove empty last line if it exists
      lines.pop();
    } else if (lines[lastLineIndex].search(/\S/) < 0) {
      // Add ellipsis to last line if it's just punctuation
      lines[lastLineIndex] += '...';
    }

    return lines;
  }, [children]);

  return <p>{textParts.map((part, index) => (index === textParts.length - 1 ? <br key={index} /> : part))}</p>;
};

TextWithNoWidows.propTypes = {
  children: PropTypes.node,
};

export default TextWithNoWidows;
```

To use this utility component, simply wrap the text content you want to process inside `<TextWithNoWidows>`:

```jsx
<TextWithNoWidows>
